# Destructive confirmations must have a red affirmative button

Slack renders a **modal's submit button in the workspace theme colour and gives
it no `style` field** — you cannot make it red. So a destructive confirmation
(delete, disband, leave, stop, reset, remove, purge) must present its affirmative
as a `danger` (red) **button inside the modal body**, run the work as a *block
action*, and then close the modal by replacing the view.

Reference implementation: the DB-reset modal (`handlers/sync.py`) and the leave/
disband/stop-sync confirmations (`handlers/group_manage.py`, `handlers/channel_sync.py`).

Do:

- Build the confirm modal with `submit_button_text=None` and `close_button_text="Cancel"`.
- Put the affirmative in an `orm.ActionsBlock` with `orm.ButtonElement(..., style="danger")`.
- Route the button's `action_id` through `ACTION_MAPPER` (block action), never `VIEW_MAPPER`.
- After the work, call `handlers._common._close_modal_done(client, body, message)` so the
  modal becomes a close-only "done" screen (block actions cannot close a modal themselves).
- Keep the id in the modal's `private_metadata` (still present on modal block actions).

```python
orm.ActionsBlock(elements=[
    orm.ButtonElement(label="Disband Group", action=actions.CONFIG_DISBAND_GROUP_CONFIRM,
                      value=str(group_id), style="danger"),
])
# routing.py: ACTION_MAPPER[actions.CONFIG_DISBAND_GROUP_CONFIRM] = handlers.handle_disband_group_confirm
```

Watch the prefix matcher: `helpers.core._PREFIXED_ACTIONS` folds any `action_id`
that starts with a trigger prefix (e.g. `leave_group`) back onto that trigger.
A confirm action must **not** share that prefix — name it `confirm_leave_group`,
not `leave_group_confirm`, or it will misroute to the modal-opening handler.

Don't add a parallel "legacy" view-submission route alongside the block action;
convert the existing route in place.
